const GST_BOOK_DATA={"7":{"passages":[{"text":"What Little Things!\nBy Madison Julius Cawein\nFrom \"One Day and Another\"\nWhat little things are those\nThat hold our happiness!\nA smile, a glance, a rose\nDropped from her hair or dress;\nA word, a look, a touch,\nThese are so much, so much.\nAn air we can't forget;\nA sunset's gold that gleams;\nA spray of mignonette,\nWill fill the soul with dreams\nMore than all history says,\nOr romance of old days.\nFor of the human heart,\nNot brain, is memory;\nThese things it makes a part\nOf its own entity;\nThe joys, the pains whereof\nAre the very food of love.\nFrom \"One Day and Another\" (1901). Public domain.","images":[]},{"text":"Mangroves: The Coast's Living Shield\nAlong many Philippine shores, where the sea meets the land, grow forests that seem to stand on stilts. These are mangroves, trees and shrubs that can live in salty water that would kill most other plants. Their tangled roots rise above the mud like the legs of a giant spider.\nMangroves are more than strange-looking trees. Their roots slow down waves and hold the soft mud in place. When a strong storm pushes seawater toward the shore, a wide belt of mangroves can weaken the waves before they reach the houses behind it. For coastal towns, this living wall can mean the difference between damage and safety.\nMangroves also feed and shelter sea life. Young fish, crabs, and shrimp hide among the roots, where bigger animals cannot easily reach them. When they grow larger, many of them swim out to the reefs and open sea. Fishers depend on these animals for food and income, so a healthy mangrove forest helps keep their nets full.\nThere is one more gift that people cannot see. Mangroves take carbon from the air and store it in their wood and in the mud below them, which helps slow the warming of the planet.\nSadly, a large part of the country's mangroves has been cut down over the past century to make room for fishponds, roads, and buildings. Today, many communities are planting mangrove seedlings again. Students, fishers, and local officials work together, because they have learned that protecting the coast begins with protecting its trees.\nWritten for READwy.","images":[]},{"text":"Reading the Heat Index\nOn a hot day, the thermometer does not tell the whole story. When the air is humid, our sweat dries more slowly, so the body cannot cool itself well. That is why PAGASA reports the heat index: how hot it actually feels to the human body when temperature and humidity are combined.\nPAGASA groups the heat index into four levels.\nCaution (27 to 32 degrees Celsius): People may feel tired after long periods of activity outdoors.\nExtreme Caution (33 to 41 degrees Celsius): Heat cramps and heat exhaustion are possible.\nDanger (42 to 51 degrees Celsius): Heat cramps and heat exhaustion are likely, and heat stroke is possible with continued exposure.\nExtreme Danger (52 degrees Celsius and above): Heat stroke is likely.\nWhat can students do? Drink water often, even before you feel thirsty. Wear light, loose clothing and use an umbrella or hat under the sun. Avoid heavy outdoor activity from late morning to mid-afternoon, when the sun is strongest. Rest in the shade when you feel dizzy, and tell a teacher or an adult right away if a classmate becomes confused, stops sweating, or faints.\nChecking the heat index each morning takes only a minute, but it can keep you and your classmates safe all day.\nWritten for READwy, based on PAGASA's public heat index classification.","images":[]},{"text":"Barangay Malinis: One Week of Household Waste\nOriginal READwy infographic. Sample data for reading practice (Barangay Malinis is a made-up example).","images":["resources/gst-book/rc/grade7-infographic.png"]}],"questions":[{"number":1,"question":"According to the speaker, what holds our happiness?","options":{"A":"great events from history","B":"old stories of romance","C":"money and fine clothes","D":"small things like a smile, a word, or a touch"},"group":0},{"number":2,"question":"\"These are so much, so much.\" What does the speaker mean?","options":{"A":"Little things are too many to count.","B":"Little things cost a lot of money.","C":"Little things mean a great deal to us.","D":"Little things are hard to carry."},"group":0},{"number":3,"question":"According to the poem, which part of us keeps memories?","options":{"A":"the heart","B":"the brain","C":"the eyes","D":"the hands"},"group":0},{"number":4,"question":"Which pair of things from the poem appeals to the sense of smell?","options":{"A":"a glance and a look","B":"a word and a touch","C":"a sunset's gold and a smile","D":"a rose and a spray of mignonette"},"group":0},{"number":5,"question":"The speaker says little things fill the soul with dreams \"more than all history says.\" This shows that the speaker values","options":{"A":"personal moments over famous events","B":"history books over poems","C":"dreams over real life","D":"travel over staying home"},"group":0},{"number":6,"question":"What is the mood of the poem?","options":{"A":"angry and bitter","B":"warm and thoughtful","C":"frightened and tense","D":"bored and tired"},"group":0},{"number":7,"question":"Why does the speaker repeat the question \"What little things are those\"?","options":{"A":"to show that he forgot the answer","B":"to complain about the little things","C":"to make the reader stop and wonder about small joys","D":"to make the poem longer"},"group":0},{"number":8,"question":"In the poem, \"entity\" most nearly means","options":{"A":"a whole self or being","B":"a door or entrance","C":"a kind of flower","D":"a short song"},"group":0},{"number":9,"question":"Which line best states the main idea of the poem?","options":{"A":"Romance of old days fills the soul.","B":"Joys and pains are forgotten quickly.","C":"History is more important than the heart.","D":"Small moments become a lasting part of who we are."},"group":0},{"number":10,"question":"Which of these experiences is most like the \"little things\" in the poem?","options":{"A":"winning a national contest","B":"buying an expensive phone","C":"reading about a famous war","D":"a friend's kind note on a hard day"},"group":0},{"number":11,"question":"What makes mangroves different from most other plants?","options":{"A":"They grow only on mountains.","B":"They have no roots.","C":"They can live in salty water.","D":"They need snow to grow."},"group":1},{"number":12,"question":"The author compares mangrove roots to \"the legs of a giant spider\" to help readers","options":{"A":"picture how the roots look","B":"feel afraid of mangroves","C":"learn about spiders","D":"count the roots"},"group":1},{"number":13,"question":"How do mangroves protect coastal towns during storms?","options":{"A":"They pump water back into the sea.","B":"They weaken the waves before the waves reach houses.","C":"They make the storm turn around.","D":"They build walls of sand."},"group":1},{"number":14,"question":"Why do young fish and crabs hide among mangrove roots?","options":{"A":"The water there is cold.","B":"They are looking for sunlight.","C":"Fishers feed them there.","D":"Bigger animals cannot easily reach them there."},"group":1},{"number":15,"question":"How do healthy mangroves help fishers?","options":{"A":"They give fishers shade for sleeping.","B":"They are sold as lumber.","C":"They make the sea calm all year.","D":"Many young sea animals grow up there and later become catch."},"group":1},{"number":16,"question":"Which \"gift\" of mangroves does the author say people cannot see?","options":{"A":"shelter for crabs","B":"protection from waves","C":"storing carbon from the air","D":"beautiful flowers"},"group":1},{"number":17,"question":"What caused the loss of many mangroves, according to the text?","options":{"A":"too much rain","B":"clearing land for fishponds, roads, and buildings","C":"fish eating the roots","D":"volcanic eruptions"},"group":1},{"number":18,"question":"How is the text mainly organized?","options":{"A":"as a story told in time order","B":"as a list of the benefits of mangroves, then a problem and a response","C":"as a comparison of two kinds of trees","D":"as step-by-step directions"},"group":1},{"number":19,"question":"In paragraph 5, a \"seedling\" is","options":{"A":"a young plant","B":"a fishing net","C":"a kind of shrimp","D":"a storm warning"},"group":1},{"number":20,"question":"What is the author's main purpose?","options":{"A":"to entertain readers with a fantasy story","B":"to sell mangrove seedlings","C":"to explain why mangroves matter and should be protected","D":"to describe how to build a fishpond"},"group":1},{"number":21,"question":"What does the heat index measure?","options":{"A":"how much rain will fall","B":"how many people are outdoors","C":"how strong the wind is","D":"how hot it actually feels when temperature and humidity are combined"},"group":2},{"number":22,"question":"Why does humid air make us feel hotter?","options":{"A":"Sweat dries more slowly, so the body cannot cool itself well.","B":"Humid air has more sunlight.","C":"Humid air makes us run faster.","D":"Humidity raises the thermometer reading."},"group":2},{"number":23,"question":"A heat index of 38 degrees Celsius belongs to which level?","options":{"A":"Caution","B":"Extreme Caution","C":"Danger","D":"Extreme Danger"},"group":2},{"number":24,"question":"At which level is heat stroke described as likely?","options":{"A":"Caution","B":"Extreme Caution","C":"Danger","D":"Extreme Danger"},"group":2},{"number":25,"question":"When should students avoid heavy outdoor activity?","options":{"A":"early in the morning","B":"from late morning to mid-afternoon","C":"after sunset","D":"only during the rainy season"},"group":2},{"number":26,"question":"The text says to drink water \"even before you feel thirsty.\" Why is this good advice?","options":{"A":"Thirst may come only after the body has already lost water.","B":"Water is cheaper in the morning.","C":"Thirsty people cannot read.","D":"It makes the heat index go down."},"group":2},{"number":27,"question":"Which sign means you should tell a teacher right away?","options":{"A":"A classmate is laughing.","B":"A classmate stops sweating or becomes confused.","C":"A classmate is drinking water.","D":"A classmate is wearing a hat."},"group":2},{"number":28,"question":"How is the middle part of the text organized?","options":{"A":"as levels from least to most dangerous","B":"as a story about a hot day","C":"as a debate between two people","D":"as a poem"},"group":2},{"number":29,"question":"What is the main purpose of this text?","options":{"A":"to describe a famous scientist","B":"to persuade readers to stay indoors forever","C":"to tell a funny story","D":"to inform readers about heat levels and how to stay safe"},"group":2},{"number":30,"question":"Your school plans a field day when the heat index is 45 degrees Celsius. What is the best advice?","options":{"A":"Hold every game at noon.","B":"Cancel water breaks to save time.","C":"Move or shorten activities, and take shade and water breaks; this is the Danger level.","D":"Wear thick jackets."},"group":2},{"number":31,"question":"What does the infographic show?","options":{"A":"the waste of one household for one year","B":"the waste of 100 households in one week (sample data)","C":"the waste of a whole city in one day","D":"the number of trucks in Barangay Malinis"},"group":3},{"number":32,"question":"Which kind of waste was the largest?","options":{"A":"paper and cardboard","B":"plastic bottles and containers","C":"food and kitchen scraps","D":"glass and metal cans"},"group":3},{"number":33,"question":"How many kilograms of waste were collected in all?","options":{"A":"420","B":"580","C":"880","D":"1,000"},"group":3},{"number":34,"question":"What fraction of all the waste was food and kitchen scraps?","options":{"A":"less than one tenth","B":"about one fourth","C":"a little less than half","D":"more than three fourths"},"group":3},{"number":35,"question":"Together, how much plastic was collected (bottles, containers, bags, and wrappers)?","options":{"A":"130 kilograms","B":"150 kilograms","C":"280 kilograms","D":"330 kilograms"},"group":3},{"number":36,"question":"According to the notes, which waste can become compost?","options":{"A":"food scraps","B":"glass","C":"plastic bags","D":"metal cans"},"group":3},{"number":37,"question":"Which waste usually ends up in the landfill?","options":{"A":"paper","B":"plastic bags and wrappers","C":"plastic bottles","D":"food scraps"},"group":3},{"number":38,"question":"If every household composted its food scraps, how much waste would be left?","options":{"A":"420 kilograms","B":"500 kilograms","C":"580 kilograms","D":"1,000 kilograms"},"group":3},{"number":39,"question":"Why does the infographic say \"sample data\"?","options":{"A":"The numbers are an example made for practice, not an official count.","B":"The data came from a laboratory.","C":"The barangay is the biggest in the country.","D":"The numbers are secret."},"group":3},{"number":40,"question":"Based on the infographic, which action would reduce Barangay Malinis's waste the most?","options":{"A":"recycling glass and cans","B":"composting food and kitchen scraps","C":"buying fewer rubber items","D":"using fewer paper plates"},"group":3}]},"8":{"passages":[{"text":"\"Hope\" is the thing with feathers\nBy Emily Dickinson (1830-1886)\n\"Hope\" is the thing with feathers -\nThat perches in the soul -\nAnd sings the tune without the words -\nAnd never stops - at all -\nAnd sweetest - in the Gale - is heard -\nAnd sore must be the storm -\nThat could abash the little Bird\nThat kept so many warm -\nI've heard it in the chillest land -\nAnd on the strangest Sea -\nYet - never - in Extremity,\nIt asked a crumb - of me.\nPublic domain.","images":[]},{"text":"Sinulog: Two Steps Forward, One Step Back\nEvery January, the streets of Cebu City fill with drums, bright costumes, and dancers who move in a rhythm older than the city's tall buildings. This is the Sinulog Festival, one of the biggest celebrations in the Philippines.\nThe festival honors the Santo Niño, an image of the Child Jesus. According to history, the image was given to the people of Cebu in 1521, when the Portuguese explorer Ferdinand Magellan arrived on the island. For centuries since then, Cebuanos have kept the Santo Niño at the heart of their faith.\nThe word sinulog is said to come from sulog, a Cebuano word for the current of a river. The dance imitates that flowing water: two steps forward and one step back, again and again, to the beat of the drums. Older devotees, especially women selling candles near the basilica, have danced this simple step for generations as a form of prayer.\nThe festival as we know it today began in the early 1980s, when the dance was turned into a grand street parade. Now, groups from schools and towns practice for months. They design costumes, create stories in dance, and compete for the best performance. Visitors from other provinces and other countries travel to Cebu just to watch.\nYet even with all the music and color, many Cebuanos say the heart of Sinulog is still the quiet prayer behind each step. For them, moving forward and stepping back is also a lesson for life: progress is not always a straight line, but faith keeps us moving.\nWritten for READwy.","images":[]},{"text":"The Arrow and the Song\nBy Henry Wadsworth Longfellow (1807-1882)\nI shot an arrow into the air,\nIt fell to earth, I knew not where;\nFor, so swiftly it flew, the sight\nCould not follow it in its flight.\nI breathed a song into the air,\nIt fell to earth, I knew not where;\nFor who has sight so keen and strong,\nThat it can follow the flight of song?\nLong, long afterward, in an oak\nI found the arrow, still unbroke;\nAnd the song, from beginning to end,\nI found again in the heart of a friend.\nPublic domain.","images":[]},{"text":"How 200 Students Get to School\nOriginal READwy infographic. Sample data for reading practice (a made-up school survey).","images":["resources/gst-book/rc/grade8-infographic.png"]}],"questions":[{"number":1,"question":"In the poem, what is \"the thing with feathers\"?","options":{"A":"a real bird","B":"a song","C":"a storm","D":"hope"},"group":0},{"number":2,"question":"Comparing hope to a bird is an example of","options":{"A":"a metaphor","B":"an onomatopoeia","C":"a rhyme scheme","D":"a title"},"group":0},{"number":3,"question":"Where does the bird of hope perch?","options":{"A":"in a tree","B":"on a ship","C":"in the soul","D":"in a cage"},"group":0},{"number":4,"question":"\"Sings the tune without the words\" suggests that hope","options":{"A":"is very loud","B":"is felt even when we cannot explain it","C":"only sings in the morning","D":"has forgotten its song"},"group":0},{"number":5,"question":"When is the song of hope \"sweetest\"?","options":{"A":"during a gale, or hard times","B":"on a sunny day","C":"at a party","D":"when we are asleep"},"group":0},{"number":6,"question":"In stanza 2, \"abash\" most nearly means","options":{"A":"to feed","B":"to praise","C":"to warm","D":"to silence or discourage"},"group":0},{"number":7,"question":"What does hope ask from the speaker in return?","options":{"A":"a song","B":"a warm place","C":"nothing at all","D":"a crumb every day"},"group":0},{"number":8,"question":"\"The chillest land\" and \"the strangest Sea\" stand for","options":{"A":"the hardest and most unfamiliar situations in life","B":"places the poet wants to visit","C":"cold weather in America","D":"a sailor's adventures"},"group":0},{"number":9,"question":"Which statement best expresses the theme of the poem?","options":{"A":"Birds need care in winter.","B":"Storms always win in the end.","C":"Hope stays with us through hard times and asks nothing in return.","D":"Songs are better without words."},"group":0},{"number":10,"question":"The many dashes in the poem make it feel","options":{"A":"like a list of numbers","B":"careful and thoughtful, with small pauses","C":"rushed and noisy","D":"like a news report"},"group":0},{"number":11,"question":"Where and when is the Sinulog Festival held?","options":{"A":"in Iloilo every December","B":"in Davao every August","C":"in Manila every June","D":"in Cebu City every January"},"group":1},{"number":12,"question":"Whom does the festival honor?","options":{"A":"Ferdinand Magellan","B":"the city's mayor","C":"the Santo Niño","D":"the best dancers"},"group":1},{"number":13,"question":"According to the text, when was the image given to the people of Cebu?","options":{"A":"in 1521","B":"in 1898","C":"in the 1980s","D":"last year"},"group":1},{"number":14,"question":"The word sinulog is said to come from a word meaning","options":{"A":"the beat of a drum","B":"a candle","C":"the current of a river","D":"a prayer"},"group":1},{"number":15,"question":"How does the dance imitate flowing water?","options":{"A":"Dancers spin in circles.","B":"Dancers move two steps forward and one step back.","C":"Dancers jump into the river.","D":"Dancers sit still."},"group":1},{"number":16,"question":"What changed in the early 1980s?","options":{"A":"The image arrived in Cebu.","B":"Drums were no longer used.","C":"The festival stopped.","D":"The dance became a grand street parade."},"group":1},{"number":17,"question":"In paragraph 3, \"devotees\" are","options":{"A":"people with strong religious devotion","B":"tourists with cameras","C":"drum makers","D":"river guides"},"group":1},{"number":18,"question":"What does the author suggest about the \"heart\" of Sinulog?","options":{"A":"It is the prize money.","B":"It is the quiet prayer behind each step.","C":"It is the costumes.","D":"It is the tall buildings."},"group":1},{"number":19,"question":"\"Progress is not always a straight line.\" How does this connect to the dance?","options":{"A":"Dancers walk in zigzags.","B":"Dancers never finish.","C":"The parade route is curved.","D":"Moving two steps forward and one back still leads forward, like life."},"group":1},{"number":20,"question":"What is the main purpose of this feature article?","options":{"A":"to describe the history and meaning of the Sinulog Festival","B":"to sell tickets to Cebu","C":"to argue against festivals","D":"to give directions to the basilica"},"group":1},{"number":21,"question":"What happened to the arrow the speaker shot?","options":{"A":"It broke in the air.","B":"It fell to earth, and he did not know where.","C":"It hit a friend.","D":"It flew back to him."},"group":2},{"number":22,"question":"Why could the speaker not follow the arrow?","options":{"A":"It flew too swiftly for the eye.","B":"It was night.","C":"He closed his eyes.","D":"It was too small to see."},"group":2},{"number":23,"question":"What does the speaker do in stanza 2?","options":{"A":"He shoots another arrow.","B":"He climbs an oak.","C":"He breathes a song into the air.","D":"He calls a friend."},"group":2},{"number":24,"question":"Where does the speaker find the arrow later?","options":{"A":"in a river","B":"in a friend's hand","C":"in his house","D":"in an oak tree"},"group":2},{"number":25,"question":"Where does he find the song again?","options":{"A":"in a book","B":"in the wind","C":"in the oak","D":"in the heart of a friend"},"group":2},{"number":26,"question":"The two stanzas begin in almost the same way. This repetition helps the reader","options":{"A":"compare the arrow and the song","B":"forget the arrow","C":"count the syllables","D":"find the title"},"group":2},{"number":27,"question":"\"Still unbroke\" tells us that the arrow","options":{"A":"was broken","B":"was not broken","C":"was lost forever","D":"was made of gold"},"group":2},{"number":28,"question":"What might the song stand for?","options":{"A":"a loud noise","B":"a type of bird","C":"a weapon","D":"kind words or good deeds we share"},"group":2},{"number":29,"question":"Which statement best expresses the message of the poem?","options":{"A":"Arrows are dangerous.","B":"Songs are hard to remember.","C":"What we share with others can stay with them for a long time.","D":"Always look where you shoot."},"group":2},{"number":30,"question":"What is the rhyme pattern of each stanza?","options":{"A":"every line rhymes with every other line","B":"no lines rhyme","C":"lines rhyme in pairs (AABB)","D":"only the last word rhymes"},"group":2},{"number":31,"question":"How many students were surveyed?","options":{"A":"70","B":"100","C":"150","D":"200"},"group":3},{"number":32,"question":"Which is the most common way of getting to school?","options":{"A":"walking","B":"jeepney","C":"tricycle","D":"family car"},"group":3},{"number":33,"question":"How many students ride a jeepney or a tricycle?","options":{"A":"50","B":"90","C":"110","D":"40"},"group":3},{"number":34,"question":"Which two ways have the same number of students?","options":{"A":"walking and jeepney","B":"tricycle and bicycle","C":"school service and bicycle","D":"family car and walking"},"group":3},{"number":35,"question":"What percent of the students walk to school?","options":{"A":"25%","B":"70%","C":"50%","D":"35%"},"group":3},{"number":36,"question":"Which way of travel is used by the fewest students?","options":{"A":"family car","B":"bicycle","C":"school service","D":"tricycle"},"group":3},{"number":37,"question":"According to the notes, which students pay a fare every day?","options":{"A":"those who walk","B":"jeepney and tricycle riders","C":"bicycle riders","D":"those in a family car"},"group":3},{"number":38,"question":"How many students travel without using any fuel?","options":{"A":"15","B":"70","C":"85","D":"100"},"group":3},{"number":39,"question":"What can you conclude from the note about students who walk?","options":{"A":"Walking is the slowest way.","B":"Many students live near the school.","C":"No one owns a bicycle.","D":"The school has no gate."},"group":3},{"number":40,"question":"The school wants fewer students to spend money on fares. Which plan fits the data best?","options":{"A":"Ask more students to ride tricycles.","B":"Make safe bike racks and walking paths for students who live nearby.","C":"Stop the school service.","D":"Close the school gate."},"group":3}]},"9":{"passages":[{"text":"The Road Not Taken\nBy Robert Frost\nTwo roads diverged in a yellow wood,\nAnd sorry I could not travel both\nAnd be one traveler, long I stood\nAnd looked down one as far as I could\nTo where it bent in the undergrowth;\nThen took the other, as just as fair,\nAnd having perhaps the better claim,\nBecause it was grassy and wanted wear;\nThough as for that the passing there\nHad worn them really about the same,\nAnd both that morning equally lay\nIn leaves no step had trodden black.\nOh, I kept the first for another day!\nYet knowing how way leads on to way,\nI doubted if I should ever come back.\nI shall be telling this with a sigh\nSomewhere ages and ages hence:\nTwo roads diverged in a wood, and I—\nI took the one less traveled by,\nAnd that has made all the difference.\nFirst published in 1916. Public domain.","images":[]},{"text":"Sleep and the Teenage Brain\nMany students treat sleep as something to cut when there is too much to do. A few more videos, one more round of a game, or a late review session seem harmless. But sleep is not wasted time. It is when the brain does some of its most important work.\nDuring sleep, the brain sorts what we learned during the day and stores it as memory. This is why a student who studies and then sleeps well often remembers more than one who stays up all night cramming. Sleep also helps the body grow, repair muscles, and fight sickness.\nTeenagers need more sleep than most adults, about eight to ten hours each night. Yet many teens get much less. Part of the reason is biology: during the teenage years, the body's inner clock shifts, so teens naturally feel sleepy later at night. When school starts early, the hours of sleep become short.\nScreens make the problem worse. The light from phones and tablets can trick the brain into thinking it is still daytime, and exciting games or messages keep the mind awake.\nThe good news is that small habits help. Going to bed and waking up at about the same time every day, even on weekends, keeps the inner clock steady. Putting the phone away at least an hour before bed, keeping the room dark and quiet, and avoiding coffee or soft drinks at night can also make falling asleep easier.\nA rested brain pays attention better, solves problems faster, and handles stress more calmly. Getting enough sleep may be the easiest way to become a better learner.\nWritten for READwy.","images":[]},{"text":"Living on the Ring of Fire\nThe Philippines lies along the Pacific Ring of Fire, a long chain of volcanoes and fault lines that circles the Pacific Ocean. Here, huge plates of the Earth's crust slowly push against, slide past, or sink beneath one another. When stress between the plates is suddenly released, the ground shakes. This is an earthquake.\nMost earthquakes in the country are too weak for people to feel, but some are strong enough to crack roads, topple buildings, and cause landslides. Because no one can predict exactly when a strong earthquake will strike, the best protection is being prepared.\nThat is why schools hold earthquake drills. The key action is easy to remember: Duck, Cover, and Hold. Drop to the ground, take cover under a sturdy table or desk, and hold on until the shaking stops. Stay away from windows, shelves, and anything that could fall. When the shaking ends, walk calmly to the open evacuation area with your class, and wait for instructions from your teacher.\nEarthquakes under the sea can also cause tsunamis, a series of powerful waves. If you live near the coast and feel a strong earthquake, see the sea suddenly pull back from the shore, or hear an unusual roar from the ocean, do not wait for an official warning. Move to higher ground right away.\nPHIVOLCS, the government agency that studies volcanoes and earthquakes, monitors the ground every day and gives advice to the public. Its work, together with families who prepare emergency kits and practice their plans, helps keep communities safe on the Ring of Fire.\nWritten for READwy, based on public earthquake safety guidance.","images":[]},{"text":"How Long Do Grade 9 Students Sleep?\nOriginal READwy infographic. Sample data for reading practice (a made-up class survey).","images":["resources/gst-book/rc/grade9-infographic.png"]}],"questions":[{"number":1,"question":"What situation does the speaker face at the beginning of the poem?","options":{"A":"He is lost at sea.","B":"He is building a new road.","C":"He is racing a friend.","D":"He must choose between two roads in a yellow wood."},"group":0},{"number":2,"question":"\"Diverged\" most nearly means","options":{"A":"split and went in different directions","B":"joined together","C":"disappeared","D":"became muddy"},"group":0},{"number":3,"question":"Why does the speaker feel sorry in the first stanza?","options":{"A":"He forgot his map.","B":"He is tired.","C":"The wood is dark.","D":"He cannot travel both roads."},"group":0},{"number":4,"question":"How does the speaker describe the two roads after looking closely?","options":{"A":"One is much longer.","B":"Both are covered in snow.","C":"One is dangerous.","D":"They are worn about the same."},"group":0},{"number":5,"question":"\"I kept the first for another day!\" shows that the speaker","options":{"A":"hopes to come back, though he doubts he will","B":"will never think about it again","C":"is angry at the first road","D":"has already walked both"},"group":0},{"number":6,"question":"Why does the speaker say he will tell this story \"with a sigh\"?","options":{"A":"He will be bored.","B":"He will think about how one choice shaped his life.","C":"He will be out of breath.","D":"He will forget the story."},"group":0},{"number":7,"question":"In the poem, the two roads stand for","options":{"A":"real highways","B":"the seasons","C":"two friends","D":"choices in life"},"group":0},{"number":8,"question":"\"And that has made all the difference\" means the choice","options":{"A":"did not matter","B":"changed the course of the speaker's life","C":"was made by someone else","D":"was a mistake"},"group":0},{"number":9,"question":"Which word best describes the speaker's mood?","options":{"A":"silly","B":"furious","C":"thoughtful","D":"terrified"},"group":0},{"number":10,"question":"Which situation is most like the one in the poem?","options":{"A":"choosing between two senior high school tracks","B":"eating breakfast","C":"reading a road sign","D":"losing a pencil"},"group":0},{"number":11,"question":"What is the main idea of the text?","options":{"A":"Games are bad for teenagers.","B":"Coffee helps students learn.","C":"School should start at noon.","D":"Sleep is important brain work, and teens need enough of it."},"group":1},{"number":12,"question":"What does the brain do with learning during sleep?","options":{"A":"It deletes it.","B":"It sorts it and stores it as memory.","C":"It turns it into dreams only.","D":"It sends it to the muscles."},"group":1},{"number":13,"question":"How many hours of sleep do teenagers need each night?","options":{"A":"about 5 to 6","B":"about 6 to 7","C":"about 8 to 10","D":"about 11 to 12"},"group":1},{"number":14,"question":"Why do teenagers naturally feel sleepy later at night?","options":{"A":"Their inner clock shifts during the teenage years.","B":"They eat too much rice.","C":"They exercise too much.","D":"Their rooms are too dark."},"group":1},{"number":15,"question":"How can screen light affect sleep?","options":{"A":"It makes the eyes stronger.","B":"It has no effect.","C":"It helps the brain rest.","D":"It tricks the brain into thinking it is still daytime."},"group":1},{"number":16,"question":"Which habit does the text recommend?","options":{"A":"Sleeping late on weekends to catch up","B":"Drinking soft drinks at night","C":"Waking up at about the same time every day","D":"Using the phone in bed"},"group":1},{"number":17,"question":"In paragraph 2, \"cramming\" means","options":{"A":"studying a lot in a short time just before a test","B":"sleeping in class","C":"eating quickly","D":"packing a bag"},"group":1},{"number":18,"question":"The text compares a student who sleeps well with one who stays up cramming to show that","options":{"A":"cramming is more fun","B":"sleep is only for young children","C":"both students fail","D":"sleep helps memory more than staying up all night"},"group":1},{"number":19,"question":"How is the text organized?","options":{"A":"problem, causes, then solutions","B":"a story told by a character","C":"a list of definitions","D":"an interview"},"group":1},{"number":20,"question":"What is the author's purpose?","options":{"A":"to entertain with a bedtime story","B":"to sell a new phone","C":"to inform and persuade teens to protect their sleep","D":"to describe a school building"},"group":1},{"number":21,"question":"What is the Pacific Ring of Fire?","options":{"A":"a festival of fireworks","B":"a chain of volcanoes and fault lines around the Pacific Ocean","C":"a type of storm","D":"a group of islands near Africa"},"group":2},{"number":22,"question":"What causes an earthquake, according to the text?","options":{"A":"heavy rain on mountains","B":"the moon pulling the sea","C":"strong winds","D":"the sudden release of stress between plates of the Earth's crust"},"group":2},{"number":23,"question":"Why does the text say being prepared is the best protection?","options":{"A":"Earthquakes never happen.","B":"No one can predict exactly when a strong earthquake will strike.","C":"Buildings never fall.","D":"Drills are fun."},"group":2},{"number":24,"question":"What does \"Duck, Cover, and Hold\" tell you to do?","options":{"A":"Run outside immediately.","B":"Stand near a window.","C":"Drop down, take cover under something sturdy, and hold on.","D":"Use the elevator."},"group":2},{"number":25,"question":"What should you do after the shaking stops?","options":{"A":"Go back to your seat and continue.","B":"Walk calmly to the evacuation area with your class.","C":"Look for your phone first.","D":"Run home alone."},"group":2},{"number":26,"question":"Which is a warning sign of a tsunami?","options":{"A":"The sea suddenly pulls back from the shore.","B":"The sky turns blue.","C":"Birds sing.","D":"The tide is normal."},"group":2},{"number":27,"question":"If you are near the coast and notice a tsunami warning sign, you should","options":{"A":"wait for an official announcement","B":"stay in a low building","C":"go to the beach to watch","D":"move to higher ground right away"},"group":2},{"number":28,"question":"What is PHIVOLCS?","options":{"A":"a school club","B":"the government agency that studies volcanoes and earthquakes","C":"a kind of fault line","D":"a weather satellite"},"group":2},{"number":29,"question":"In paragraph 2, \"topple\" means","options":{"A":"to fall over","B":"to paint","C":"to grow taller","D":"to clean"},"group":2},{"number":30,"question":"What is the main purpose of the text?","options":{"A":"to explain why earthquakes happen and how to stay safe","B":"to describe a vacation","C":"to compare two countries","D":"to tell a myth about volcanoes"},"group":2},{"number":31,"question":"How many students took part in the survey?","options":{"A":"15","B":"30","C":"50","D":"100"},"group":3},{"number":32,"question":"What is the most common amount of sleep in the class?","options":{"A":"6 hours","B":"8 hours","C":"7 hours","D":"9 hours or more"},"group":3},{"number":33,"question":"How many students sleep 6 hours or less?","options":{"A":"6","B":"12","C":"18","D":"27"},"group":3},{"number":34,"question":"How many students get the 8 or more hours teenagers need?","options":{"A":"11","B":"17","C":"26","D":"33"},"group":3},{"number":35,"question":"What percent of the class gets less than 8 hours of sleep?","options":{"A":"34%","B":"50%","C":"66%","D":"90%"},"group":3},{"number":36,"question":"Which two groups have the same number of students?","options":{"A":"6 hours and 8 hours","B":"6 hours and 7 hours","C":"7 hours and 8 hours","D":"5 hours or less and 9 hours or more"},"group":3},{"number":37,"question":"According to the notes, what do students who sleep 6 hours or less often say?","options":{"A":"They feel sleepy in class.","B":"They get the best grades.","C":"They never use phones.","D":"They sleep too much."},"group":3},{"number":38,"question":"Which text in this set best explains the results in the infographic?","options":{"A":"The Road Not Taken","B":"Sleep and the Teenage Brain","C":"Living on the Ring of Fire","D":"none of them"},"group":3},{"number":39,"question":"Which conclusion is best supported by the infographic?","options":{"A":"Everyone in the class sleeps enough.","B":"Most of the class sleeps less than teenagers need.","C":"No one uses a phone at night.","D":"Students sleep more on school nights than adults."},"group":3},{"number":40,"question":"Which advice would most likely help this class sleep longer?","options":{"A":"Study until midnight.","B":"Drink soft drinks at night.","C":"Put phones away an hour before bed.","D":"Sleep at a different time every day."},"group":3}]},"10":{"passages":[{"text":"If—\nBy Rudyard Kipling (1865-1936), first published in 1910\nIf you can keep your head when all about you\n    Are losing theirs and blaming it on you,\nIf you can trust yourself when all men doubt you,\n    But make allowance for their doubting too;\nIf you can wait and not be tired by waiting,\n    Or being lied about, don't deal in lies,\nOr being hated, don't give way to hating,\n    And yet don't look too good, nor talk too wise:\nIf you can dream—and not make dreams your master;\n    If you can think—and not make thoughts your aim;\nIf you can meet with Triumph and Disaster\n    And treat those two impostors just the same;\nIf you can bear to hear the truth you've spoken\n    Twisted by knaves to make a trap for fools,\nOr watch the things you gave your life to, broken,\n    And stoop and build 'em up with worn-out tools:\nIf you can make one heap of all your winnings\n    And risk it on one turn of pitch-and-toss,\nAnd lose, and start again at your beginnings\n    And never breathe a word about your loss;\nIf you can force your heart and nerve and sinew\n    To serve your turn long after they are gone,\nAnd so hold on when there is nothing in you\n    Except the Will which says to them: 'Hold on!'\nIf you can talk with crowds and keep your virtue,\n    Or walk with Kings—nor lose the common touch,\nIf neither foes nor loving friends can hurt you,\n    If all men count with you, but none too much;\nIf you can fill the unforgiving minute\n    With sixty seconds' worth of distance run,\nYours is the Earth and everything that's in it,\n    And—which is more—you'll be a Man, my son!\nPublic domain.","images":[]},{"text":"Asking for Help Is a Strength\nWe take care of our bodies when we are sick: we rest, see a doctor, and take medicine. Our minds deserve the same care. Mental health is how we think, feel, and cope with daily life, and like physical health, it can be strong on some days and weak on others.\nEveryone feels sad, worried, or stressed sometimes. Exams, problems at home, changes in friendships, and worries about the future can weigh on any student. These feelings are normal. But when sadness or worry stays for weeks, makes it hard to sleep, eat, study, or enjoy things we used to love, it may be a sign that we need extra help.\nMany young people stay silent because they fear being judged or called weak. In fact, the opposite is true. Recognizing that something is wrong and reaching out takes courage. Talking to a trusted adult, such as a parent, a teacher, or the school's guidance counselor, is often the first and most important step.\nFriends can help too. If a classmate seems withdrawn or troubled, a simple \"Are you okay? I'm here to listen\" can mean a lot. Listening without judging, staying with them, and encouraging them to talk to an adult is a real act of care. If someone talks about harming themselves, tell an adult right away, even if they asked you to keep it a secret.\nSmall daily habits also protect mental health: getting enough sleep, moving our bodies, spending time with people who make us feel safe, and taking breaks from social media.\nNo one has to carry heavy feelings alone. Asking for help is not a sign of weakness. It is a sign of strength, and the beginning of feeling better.\nWritten for READwy.","images":[]},{"text":"Arachne, the Weaver\nLong ago in the land of Lydia lived a young woman named Arachne. Her family was poor, but her hands were gifted. When she wove, the threads seemed to come alive: rivers rippled across her cloth, and birds looked ready to fly out of it. People traveled from far away just to watch her work.\n\"Surely,\" the visitors whispered, \"the goddess Athena herself must have taught you.\" Athena was the goddess of wisdom and of weaving, and to be called her student was a great honor. But Arachne tossed her head. \"No one taught me,\" she said. \"My skill is my own. I could weave better than Athena herself. Let her come and try me!\"\nOne day an old woman leaning on a stick came to Arachne's door. \"Child,\" she said kindly, \"be proud of your gift, but do not insult the gods. Ask Athena's pardon, and she will forgive you.\" Arachne laughed. \"Keep your advice, old woman. If Athena is not afraid, let her face me.\"\nAt once the old woman stood tall and shining. She was Athena. The visitors fell to their knees, but Arachne only grew pale for a moment, then set up her loom. The contest began.\nAthena wove the gods in their glory, and in each corner she showed humans who had been punished for challenging them, as a warning. Arachne wove scenes that mocked the gods, showing their mistakes and tricks. Her work was flawless, and that made it worse. Even Athena could find no fault in the weaving, only in its pride.\nIn anger, Athena tore the cloth apart. Then she touched Arachne and said, \"Since you love to weave, you and your children shall weave forever.\" Arachne's body shrank, her arms became thin legs, and she became the first spider. To this day, her descendants spin their delicate webs, still weaving, still proud.\nAn ancient Greek myth, retold for READwy.","images":[]},{"text":"A Rice Farm Cooperative's Harvests\nOriginal READwy infographic. Sample data for reading practice (a made-up farmers' cooperative).","images":["resources/gst-book/rc/grade10-infographic.png"]}],"questions":[{"number":1,"question":"What is the speaker of the poem doing?","options":{"A":"describing a battle","B":"complaining about the king","C":"telling a love story","D":"giving a son advice about how to become a mature person"},"group":0},{"number":2,"question":"\"Keep your head when all about you / Are losing theirs\" means","options":{"A":"stay calm when others panic","B":"wear a helmet","C":"agree with everyone","D":"hide from people"},"group":0},{"number":3,"question":"Why does the speaker call Triumph and Disaster \"two impostors\"?","options":{"A":"Both are fake friends.","B":"Neither success nor failure is as permanent or important as it seems.","C":"They are two thieves.","D":"They are names of kings."},"group":0},{"number":4,"question":"\"Stoop and build 'em up with worn-out tools\" shows","options":{"A":"carelessness","B":"laziness","C":"perseverance after loss","D":"a love of building"},"group":0},{"number":5,"question":"\"Walk with Kings—nor lose the common touch\" means","options":{"A":"only spend time with powerful people","B":"avoid crowds","C":"become a king","D":"stay humble and kind to ordinary people even among the powerful"},"group":0},{"number":6,"question":"In stanza 3, \"sinew\" refers to","options":{"A":"strength of the body (tendons)","B":"money","C":"a kind of game","D":"a song"},"group":0},{"number":7,"question":"Why does nearly every line begin with \"If\"?","options":{"A":"to build a long list of conditions toward one powerful ending","B":"to show the speaker is unsure","C":"to ask questions","D":"to confuse the reader"},"group":0},{"number":8,"question":"\"Fill the unforgiving minute / With sixty seconds' worth of distance run\" advises the reader to","options":{"A":"run every day","B":"use time fully and wisely","C":"forgive others quickly","D":"count the seconds"},"group":0},{"number":9,"question":"Which quality does the poem value most?","options":{"A":"self-control and resilience","B":"wealth","C":"fame","D":"being liked by everyone"},"group":0},{"number":10,"question":"Which student best shows the attitude in the poem?","options":{"A":"One who quits after failing a quiz","B":"One who boasts after winning","C":"One who stays calm, learns from a loss, and tries again","D":"One who blames classmates"},"group":0},{"number":11,"question":"How does the text define mental health?","options":{"A":"how strong our muscles are","B":"how well we sleep","C":"how many friends we have","D":"how we think, feel, and cope with daily life"},"group":1},{"number":12,"question":"Why does the author compare mental health to physical health?","options":{"A":"to describe exercise","B":"to say doctors are expensive","C":"to explain medicine","D":"to show that both need care"},"group":1},{"number":13,"question":"When might sadness or worry be a sign that we need extra help?","options":{"A":"when it lasts a few minutes","B":"when it stays for weeks and makes daily life hard","C":"after watching a sad movie","D":"before every exam"},"group":1},{"number":14,"question":"Why do many young people stay silent?","options":{"A":"They fear being judged or called weak.","B":"They have no problems.","C":"They are too busy.","D":"They do not know any adults."},"group":1},{"number":15,"question":"According to the text, what is often the first and most important step?","options":{"A":"posting about it online","B":"keeping it secret","C":"staying home from school","D":"talking to a trusted adult"},"group":1},{"number":16,"question":"What should you do if a friend talks about harming themselves?","options":{"A":"Keep the secret.","B":"Tell an adult right away.","C":"Change the topic.","D":"Ignore it."},"group":1},{"number":17,"question":"In paragraph 4, \"withdrawn\" most nearly means","options":{"A":"quiet and keeping away from others","B":"very talkative","C":"late for class","D":"happy and excited"},"group":1},{"number":18,"question":"Which is NOT one of the daily habits the text suggests?","options":{"A":"getting enough sleep","B":"moving our bodies","C":"spending more time on social media","D":"spending time with people who make us feel safe"},"group":1},{"number":19,"question":"Which sentence states the author's main claim?","options":{"A":"\"Everyone feels sad, worried, or stressed sometimes.\"","B":"\"Asking for help is not a sign of weakness. It is a sign of strength.\"","C":"\"Exams can weigh on any student.\"","D":"\"Friends can help too.\""},"group":1},{"number":20,"question":"What is the author's purpose?","options":{"A":"to entertain","B":"to describe a hospital","C":"to sell medicine","D":"to inform readers about mental health and encourage them to seek help"},"group":1},{"number":21,"question":"Why did people travel to see Arachne?","options":{"A":"She was a queen.","B":"Her weaving was wonderfully skilled.","C":"She told stories.","D":"She sold spiders."},"group":2},{"number":22,"question":"How did Arachne respond when people said Athena must have taught her?","options":{"A":"She thanked Athena.","B":"She said her skill was her own and she could beat Athena.","C":"She stopped weaving.","D":"She asked to meet her teacher."},"group":2},{"number":23,"question":"Who was the old woman with the stick?","options":{"A":"Arachne's mother","B":"a neighbor","C":"Athena in disguise","D":"a weaver from another town"},"group":2},{"number":24,"question":"What did the old woman advise Arachne to do?","options":{"A":"weave faster","B":"teach others","C":"leave Lydia","D":"ask Athena's pardon"},"group":2},{"number":25,"question":"What did Athena weave in the corners of her cloth?","options":{"A":"rivers and birds","B":"humans punished for challenging the gods","C":"Arachne's family","D":"spiders"},"group":2},{"number":26,"question":"Why did Arachne's flawless work make things \"worse\"?","options":{"A":"It took too long.","B":"It used too much thread.","C":"It was ugly.","D":"Its perfection made her disrespect toward the gods even harder to forgive."},"group":2},{"number":27,"question":"What happened to Arachne at the end?","options":{"A":"She became a goddess.","B":"She won a prize.","C":"She became the first spider.","D":"She moved away."},"group":2},{"number":28,"question":"This myth explains the origin of","options":{"A":"rivers","B":"spiders and their webs","C":"looms","D":"the goddess Athena"},"group":2},{"number":29,"question":"Which character trait leads to Arachne's downfall?","options":{"A":"kindness","B":"laziness","C":"pride","D":"fear"},"group":2},{"number":30,"question":"Which lesson does the myth teach?","options":{"A":"Talent should be shown with humility.","B":"Never learn to weave.","C":"Old people are always gods.","D":"Contests are unfair."},"group":2},{"number":31,"question":"What does the infographic show?","options":{"A":"rice prices in markets","B":"rainfall each month","C":"the number of farmers","D":"the cooperative's average wet-season harvest per hectare (sample data)"},"group":3},{"number":32,"question":"In which year was the harvest lowest?","options":{"A":"2021","B":"2022","C":"2023","D":"2025"},"group":3},{"number":33,"question":"What explains the low harvest in that year?","options":{"A":"a strong typhoon flooded the fields","B":"too much sunlight","C":"new seeds","D":"fewer farmers"},"group":3},{"number":34,"question":"How much did the harvest increase from 2023 to 2024?","options":{"A":"6 sacks per hectare","B":"16 sacks per hectare","C":"26 sacks per hectare","D":"96 sacks per hectare"},"group":3},{"number":35,"question":"What change in 2024 may explain the better harvests after the typhoon year?","options":{"A":"no rain at all","B":"planting fewer fields","C":"flood-tolerant seeds and better drainage","D":"selling the farm"},"group":3},{"number":36,"question":"How many kilograms of palay is a harvest of 100 sacks?","options":{"A":"500 kilograms","B":"1,000 kilograms","C":"5,000 kilograms","D":"50,000 kilograms"},"group":3},{"number":37,"question":"Which year had the highest harvest?","options":{"A":"2021","B":"2022","C":"2024","D":"2025"},"group":3},{"number":38,"question":"Between which two years did the harvest increase by 5 sacks per hectare?","options":{"A":"2021 and 2022","B":"2022 and 2023","C":"2023 and 2024","D":"2024 and 2025"},"group":3},{"number":39,"question":"Which statement is best supported by the data?","options":{"A":"The harvest went down every year.","B":"After one bad year, the harvests recovered and reached a new high.","C":"Typhoons never affect rice farms.","D":"The harvest stayed exactly the same."},"group":3},{"number":40,"question":"Why does the infographic say \"sample data\"?","options":{"A":"The numbers are an example made for practice, not an official report.","B":"The numbers come from the Department of Agriculture.","C":"The cooperative is the largest in Asia.","D":"The data is secret."},"group":3}]}};
